// ============================================================
// TODO - residuals that drive themselves out
// ============================================================
// A `// TODO:` comment is a promise with no way to collect on it. It cannot
// fail, cannot be counted, cannot be assigned, and cannot tell you it came true
// while nobody was looking. Measured 2026-08-11: 128 of them in this repo (88
// in src/, 40 in koru_std/), every one invisible to everything but grep.
//
// This module makes a residual DRIVABLE, which is a different and much narrower
// claim than making it visible. The distinction is the whole design:
//
//   WHAT MAKES A RESIDUAL DRIVABLE IS NOT THAT IT IS DECLARED. IT IS THAT
//   SOMETHING CAN RUN AND DECIDE IT.
//
// The proof already sits in this repo. 66 regression tests carry a `TODO`
// marker file, and scripts/todo_sweep.sh drives every one of them without
// reading a single word of their prose — it runs them and reads the verdict.
// That works because a parked test HAS A BODY. A comment does not, so no
// amount of declaring one makes it drivable; it only makes it tidy.
//
// So a declaration here is not a nicer comment. It is the act of GIVING A
// RESIDUAL A BODY, and the module refuses declarations that fail to.
//
// TWO DISPOSITIONS, TWO DIFFERENT CLOSERS. They differ by who can close
// them, and — as with std/invariants — the NAME carries the bit, because you
// cannot recover it by re-reading afterwards. "Implement cross-flow
// optimization" reads like a build task and is actually a ruling; nothing in
// the text says so, and the person who knew has moved on.
//
//   owed     — someone must BUILD this. It names a witness: a regression test
//              that is red today and green when the residual is discharged.
//              The sweep drives it with no human in the loop. Growth is debt.
//
//   doubted  — someone must RULE on this. No amount of building closes it,
//              because the open question is whether the thing should exist at
//              all. It names the question and what evicts if the answer is no.
//              A machine can only surface it. Growth means unanswered
//              questions accumulating, which is a different illness from debt
//              and wants a different medicine.
//
// The gradients are opposite in the way that lets a gate judge the pile
// without understanding any entry in it: `owed` shrinks by writing code,
// `doubted` shrinks by someone answering. A pile that only ever grows on the
// `doubted` side is not a backlog, it is a queue of decisions nobody is making.
//
// ============================================================
// A WITNESS IS MANDATORY, AND THIS IS THE POINT OF THE MODULE
// ============================================================
// An `owed` residual MUST name a test, and the reader below exits non-zero if
// that test does not exist or pins nothing. That refusal is not pedantry — it
// is the entire safety property, and it was bought with a measured failure.
//
// The first run of scripts/todo_sweep.sh found three parked tests passing and
// NONE of them promotable: two asserted only that the program exited zero, and
// both printed the exact error they were parked on. A driver pointed at
// residuals with no real assertion does not close them, it manufactures
// victories — green-by-edit with a work queue attached.
//
// So the entry fee is a failing test, and it doubles as an honesty filter: a
// residual you cannot write a red test for is one you do not yet understand
// well enough to declare. Leave it a comment until you do.
//
// ============================================================
// EVERY FIELD HERE IS READ BY THE READER BELOW
// ============================================================
// Deliberate, and a correction of a live defect in the sibling module: the
// three rules in invariants/invariants.kz each carry a `"check"` command, the
// reader never prints it, and nothing runs it. A declared field with no
// consumer is a residual wearing the costume of a feature. If a field is added
// here it gets printed or it gets refused.
//
// Usage — a JSON block, exactly as flag.declare and std/invariants take one:
//
//   std/todo:owed {
//     "name": "serializer-drops-branches",
//     "site": "src/ast_serializer.zig:1325",
//     "owed": "The serializer writes an empty branch list for every
//              conditional, so a program round-tripped through it comes back
//              with its branches gone. Silent wrong output, no diagnostic.",
//     "witness": "395_020_serializer_round_trips_a_conditional",
//     "tags": ["silent-wrong-output"]
//   }
//
//   std/todo:doubted {
//     "name": "optimizer-layer",
//     "question": "Does an optimizer layer exist in this compiler, or not?",
//     "evicts": "21 residuals and their dead stubs: nine slots in
//               src/compiler_coordinator.zig that take a program and return
//               false, four emit sites writing a comment where a fused or
//               inlined event belongs, and the loop and kernel notes in
//               koru_std/optimizations/.",
//     "tags": ["ruling"]
//   }
//
// `name` is the identity — nothing else is. `site` says where the residual
// physically lives, because unlike an invariant (a claim about the whole
// program) a residual is always about ONE PLACE. `tags` route it to consumers.
//
// In pure `.k` there is no tilde — this is a Koru feature, not a
// host-embedding one. A host-embedding file writes the same block with ``.
//
// [norun] for the same reason as flag.declare and std/invariants: the
// declaration never executes. It is data in the AST, harvested after the parse
// by whoever asks. That is what makes the surface metacircular — a consumer
// discovers residuals by parsing the program, never from a hardcoded list.

~[comptime|norun]pub tor owed { source: Source }

~[comptime|norun]pub tor doubted { source: Source }

~import std/compiler

~[comptime]

// ============================================================
// TODO COMMAND - the module ships its own reader, and its own refusal
// ============================================================
// `koruc <file> todo` lists every residual the program declares, including
// those declared by imported libraries (`ast.allFlows` descends through module
// declarations, so a library's residuals are visible rather than silently
// dropped).
//
// It exits non-zero when an `owed` residual's witness is missing or vacuous.
// That is what makes this a gate and not a listing: a consumer that greens on
// an unwitnessed residual checks nothing, which is the failure the sweep hit
// on its first run.

~std/compiler:command.declare {
    "name": "todo",
    "description": "List the residuals a program declares, and refuse unwitnessed ones"
}

~[comptime|command]pub tor todo {
    program: *const Program,
    allocator: __koru_std.mem.Allocator,
    argv: []const []const u8,
}

~proc todo|zig {
    const std = @import("std");

    // `todo` takes no arguments, for the same reason `invariants` takes none:
    // this command's output and exit code are what a gate consumes, so a caller
    // holding a spelling this module never had must get a non-zero exit rather
    // than a listing that looks like agreement.
    if (argv.len > 0) {
        std.debug.print("error: `todo` takes no arguments (got `{s}`)\n", .{argv[0]});
        std.debug.print("A gate is a consumer of the listing, not a subcommand: run\n", .{});
        std.debug.print("    koruc <file> todo\n", .{});
        std.debug.print("and act on its output. The surface is specified in koru_std/todo.kz.\n", .{});
        std.process.exit(1);
    }

    const helpers = struct {
        // Pull one `"field": "value"` out of a declaration block. Returns an
        // empty slice when absent, so a malformed block degrades to a visibly
        // incomplete row rather than a crash. Same extraction as
        // koru_std/invariants.kz — deliberately, so the two surfaces cannot
        // drift into accepting different JSON.
        fn jsonField(source: []const u8, field: []const u8) []const u8 {
            var needle_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
            const needle = std.fmt.bufPrint(&needle_buf, "\"{s}\"", .{field}) catch return "";
            const at = std.mem.indexOf(u8, source, needle) orelse return "";
            var i = at + needle.len;
            while (i < source.len and (source[i] == ' ' or source[i] == ':')) : (i += 1) {}
            if (i >= source.len) return "";
            if (source[i] == '"') {
                i += 1;
                const start = i;
                while (i < source.len and source[i] != '"') : (i += 1) {}
                return source[start..i];
            }
            if (source[i] == '[') {
                const start = i;
                while (i < source.len and source[i] != ']') : (i += 1) {}
                if (i < source.len) i += 1;
                return source[start..i];
            }
            return "";
        }

        // Find the test corpus by walking UP from the working directory.
        //
        // The reader must work from wherever it is invoked: from the directory
        // the manifest lives in, from a regression test directory six levels
        // down, and from a consumer project that is not this repo. A path
        // relative to any one of those is wrong in the other two, and a reader
        // that silently failed to locate the corpus would report every witness
        // as NOT FOUND — a red gate that means nothing, which is worse than no
        // gate at all.
        //
        // So: climb until a `tests` directory appears, and if none does, say so
        // and refuse. A manifest declaring owed residuals with no corpus to
        // check them against has not been verified, and calling it clean would
        // be a substitute result standing in for a check that never ran.
        fn findCorpus(alloc: std.mem.Allocator) ?[]const u8 {
            var rel = std.ArrayList(u8){ .items = &.{}, .capacity = 0 };
            defer rel.deinit(alloc);
            var depth: usize = 0;
            while (depth < 8) : (depth += 1) {
                var buf: [std.fs.max_path_bytes]u8 = undefined;
                const candidate = std.fmt.bufPrint(&buf, "{s}tests", .{rel.items}) catch return null;
                if (std.fs.cwd().openDir(candidate, .{})) |d| {
                    var dd = d;
                    dd.close();
                    return alloc.dupe(u8, candidate) catch null;
                } else |_| {}
                rel.appendSlice(alloc, "../") catch return null;
            }
            return null;
        }

        // Find a regression test directory by name under a root. Returns the
        // full path, caller frees. Null when no such test exists — which for
        // an `owed` residual is a refusal, not a warning.
        fn findTest(alloc: std.mem.Allocator, root: []const u8, name: []const u8) ?[]const u8 {
            var dir = std.fs.cwd().openDir(root, .{ .iterate = true }) catch return null;
            defer dir.close();
            var walker = dir.walk(alloc) catch return null;
            defer walker.deinit();
            while (walker.next() catch null) |entry| {
                if (entry.kind != .directory) continue;
                if (!std.mem.eql(u8, std.fs.path.basename(entry.path), name)) continue;
                return std.fs.path.join(alloc, &.{ root, entry.path }) catch null;
            }
            return null;
        }

        // Does this test assert anything? Mirrors pins_something() in
        // scripts/todo_sweep.sh rather than inventing a second predicate — two
        // predicates for one question is how a gate starts disagreeing with the
        // sweep it is supposed to feed.
        fn pinsSomething(path: []const u8) bool {
            const files = [_][]const u8{
                "expected.txt",     "expected_error.txt",
                "expected_patterns.txt", "expected_comptime.txt",
                "post.sh",
            };
            var buf: [std.fs.max_path_bytes]u8 = undefined;
            for (files) |f| {
                const p = std.fmt.bufPrint(&buf, "{s}/{s}", .{ path, f }) catch continue;
                const st = std.fs.cwd().statFile(p) catch continue;
                if (st.size > 0) return true;
            }
            // An EXPECT file counts only when it carries a real assertion
            // line; an EXPECT naming just a mode (MUST_RUN with nothing else)
            // passes on ANY output, which is exactly the vacuous case.
            const p = std.fmt.bufPrint(&buf, "{s}/EXPECT", .{path}) catch return false;
            const f = std.fs.cwd().openFile(p, .{}) catch return false;
            defer f.close();
            var rbuf: [8192]u8 = undefined;
            const n = f.readAll(&rbuf) catch return false;
            const text = rbuf[0..n];
            for ([_][]const u8{ "CONTAINS ", "NOT_CONTAINS ", "STDOUT_CONTAINS:", "ERROR_AT " }) |marker| {
                if (std.mem.indexOf(u8, text, marker) != null) return true;
            }
            return false;
        }
    };

    const ast_todo = @import("ast");
    const all_flows = ast_todo.allFlows(allocator, program);
    defer allocator.free(all_flows);

    // Located by climbing, never assumed — see findCorpus. A manifest whose
    // corpus cannot be found is unverifiable, and unverifiable is refused
    // rather than reported clean.
    const tests_root = helpers.findCorpus(allocator) orelse {
        std.debug.print("error: no test corpus found.\n", .{});
        std.debug.print("An owed residual is decided by a test, so this command climbs from the\n", .{});
        std.debug.print("working directory looking for a `tests` directory and found none within\n", .{});
        std.debug.print("eight levels. Run it from inside the project whose residuals it declares.\n", .{});
        std.process.exit(1);
    };
    defer allocator.free(tests_root);

    var owed_n: usize = 0;
    var doubted_n: usize = 0;
    var unwitnessed: usize = 0;

    // Two passes so each disposition prints as a block. They close by
    // different means and reading them interleaved hides that.
    for ([_][]const u8{ "owed", "doubted" }) |kind| {
        var printed_header = false;
        for (all_flows) |flow| {
            const mq = flow.inv().path.module_qualifier orelse continue;
            if (!std.mem.eql(u8, mq, "std.todo")) continue;
            if (flow.inv().path.segments.len != 1) continue;
            if (!std.mem.eql(u8, flow.inv().path.segments[0], kind)) continue;

            for (flow.inv().args) |arg| {
                if (!std.mem.eql(u8, arg.name, "source")) continue;
                const name = helpers.jsonField(arg.value, "name");
                const tags = helpers.jsonField(arg.value, "tags");

                if (!printed_header) {
                    std.debug.print("\n{s}\n", .{kind});
                    printed_header = true;
                }
                std.debug.print("  {s}", .{name});
                if (tags.len > 0) std.debug.print("  {s}", .{tags});
                std.debug.print("\n    declared at {s}:{d}\n", .{ flow.location.file, flow.location.line });

                if (std.mem.eql(u8, kind, "owed")) {
                    owed_n += 1;
                    const site = helpers.jsonField(arg.value, "site");
                    const what = helpers.jsonField(arg.value, "owed");
                    const witness = helpers.jsonField(arg.value, "witness");
                    if (site.len > 0) std.debug.print("    site     {s}\n", .{site});
                    if (what.len > 0) std.debug.print("    owed     {s}\n", .{what});

                    // The refusal. An owed residual with no runnable witness is
                    // a comment with extra steps, and shipping it would rebuild
                    // the exact problem this module exists to remove.
                    if (witness.len == 0) {
                        std.debug.print("    witness  MISSING — no test named; this cannot be driven\n", .{});
                        unwitnessed += 1;
                    } else if (helpers.findTest(allocator, tests_root, witness)) |path| {
                        defer allocator.free(path);
                        if (helpers.pinsSomething(path)) {
                            std.debug.print("    witness  {s}\n", .{witness});
                        } else {
                            std.debug.print("    witness  VACUOUS — {s} exists but asserts nothing\n", .{witness});
                            unwitnessed += 1;
                        }
                    } else {
                        std.debug.print("    witness  NOT FOUND — no test named {s}\n", .{witness});
                        unwitnessed += 1;
                    }
                } else {
                    doubted_n += 1;
                    const question = helpers.jsonField(arg.value, "question");
                    const evicts = helpers.jsonField(arg.value, "evicts");
                    if (question.len > 0) std.debug.print("    asks     {s}\n", .{question});
                    if (evicts.len > 0) std.debug.print("    evicts   {s}\n", .{evicts});
                }
            }
        }
    }

    if (owed_n == 0 and doubted_n == 0) {
        std.debug.print("no residuals declared\n", .{});
        return;
    }

    std.debug.print("\n{d} owed, {d} doubted\n", .{ owed_n, doubted_n });

    if (unwitnessed > 0) {
        std.debug.print("\n{d} owed residual(s) cannot be driven.\n", .{unwitnessed});
        std.debug.print("An owed residual names a test that is red today and green when it is\n", .{});
        std.debug.print("discharged. Without one, nothing can ever decide it — which is the\n", .{});
        std.debug.print("condition this module exists to refuse. Write the failing test, or\n", .{});
        std.debug.print("leave the residual as a comment until you understand it well enough to.\n", .{});
        std.process.exit(1);
    }
}
